  tasks / checklists   117 tasks, all checked — T001-T052 implementation,
                       T053-T117 bug tasks. Checklists 120/120.

Audited against project-instructions.md **v1.2.8**. The prior audit named v1.2.4,
superseded in flight by four revisions of a new Temporary Files rule — the last
of which withdrew part of an earlier one. Governance required the compliance gate
to be re-run before this phase gate; it was re-run twice, at v1.2.7 (T074) and at
v1.2.8 (`plan.md:30-31`). `spec.md`'s Compliance Check carries a supersession
note pointing at that record (T085).

## What ten iterations of QC found

65 defects beyond the 52 implementation tasks. Of the 52 raised after iteration 1:
four were faults in code or configuration — T066 put a field on the wire that
FR-029 forbids, T075 left pytest writing to the machine's shared temp directory,
T083 failed the CI format gate, T096 made the manual test procedure unrunnable.
Five more were tests that did not measure or say what they claimed — T067, T069,
T073, T079, and T115, a conformance docstring contradicting itself on the count
it exists to justify. The remaining 43 were accuracy defects in what the feature
published about itself.

The recurring shape, in every iteration: **a bug task filed against N sites gets
fixed at the one site its title names.** T071 named three task entries and left
five in plan.md; T072 named two sites and closed one; T073 fixed an assertion's
weight half and left its size half; T091 fixed the four items its title listed
and left the line that decided whether the file could be run at all; T098's fix
swept plan.md and left the same claim in tasks.md; T103 counted four sites and
reached three; T114 restored the QC record its title named and left the one the
same commit had just made missing (T116). The lesson each time was that the class is wider than the title,
and the discipline that works is sweeping the class by measurement rather than
editing the site that was named.

Per-iteration findings, severities and measurements are in `qc-report.md`, one
section per iteration. Appending that section is part of closing an iteration
rather than follow-up to it — five iterations in a row omitted one when it was
treated as follow-up (T095, T105, T110, T114, T116).

## Reported, outside E010's scope

  SC-017 and SC-018 are measured against the frozen fixture's 16 lines rather
  than the working population the spec's Assumptions state. Recorded in
  plan.md:186 in Principle VII's four-part form. Note the alternative is not
  simply "use E005's data": that set holds 199 lines of which only **24 are
  open**, and FR-022 excludes the other 175 from the worklist, so it reaches 24
  rows rather than ~200. Closing this needs a generated open-line population at
  the assumed scale.

  pip-audit has produced no result on either side: it cannot run locally (TLS
  interception) and its CI step has not executed yet. Recorded as unmeasured
  rather than as a null result.

  The security limitation's reversal trigger has fired for /src/web — 3 high
  advisories on production dependencies (next, postcss, sharp). Upgrading `next`
  across a major is E001 scaffold work with its own verification surface.

  eslint's 2 warnings, neither in a file E010 owns: an unused `readFileSync` in
  `src/web/__tests__/boundary.test.ts` (E001's file, never modified by E010), and
  an unused directive inside the generated `src/web/coverage/` output. That
  directory is skipped by git and by Prettier via `src/web/.gitignore:14`, which
  Prettier reads by default, but `eslint.config.mjs`'s `globalIgnores` does not
  list it — so it is linted whenever a coverage run precedes a lint run. Both are
  E001 toolchain configuration.

  On a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`, `npx prettier --check .`
  reports files as unformatted that are clean as stored in git (`git show
  HEAD:<path> | npx prettier --check --stdin-filepath <path>` passes). The
  difference is CRLF in the working tree against the LF that is committed, so the
  CI gate on Linux is unaffected. Recorded so the next reader does not chase it.

  **This does not extend to `ruff format --check`**, and an earlier version of
  this section said it did. Ruff defaults to `line-ending = "auto"`, which
  preserves each file's existing endings, and no override exists in this
  repository — measured here, `ruff format --check` reports zero files across all
  four tiers on the same CRLF working tree. So a ruff format failure is a real
  defect rather than a Windows artifact. T083 was exactly that: a root format
  failure on an E010-owned file that failed CI's `Format check (Python)` step, and
  its record says so explicitly. A sentence excusing that class in advance is
  worse than no sentence.
